#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172722} :activity/id #uuid "5ba01719-f8b9-5679-8758-86e56046fcdf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050936 :topic/id #uuid "72cbcd7e-0332-5490-aae5-4381fdc60e68" :topic/math-academy-id 3300} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172722 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173212 :tutorial/content "Let's now discuss how to construct a confidence interval for the proportion of the population whose members have a particular characteristic.\n\nAs a concrete example, let $p$ represent the proportion of the United States voting population that will vote for candidate $A$ in the next presidential election.\n\nSuppose we conduct a random sample of size $n=40$ from our population. After processing our results, we find that the proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ of sample members that plan to vote for candidate $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = 0.3\n$$\n\nThis is our *estimate* of the population proportion $p$.\n\nOf course, the quantity that we're *really* interested in is $p$, the proportion of the entire voting population that will vote for candidate $A$. We want to get an idea of how accurate our estimate is. Therefore, we wish to construct a confidence interval of the form\n\n$$\n(\\widehat{\\,p}-E, \\: \\widehat{\\,p}+E)\n$$\n\nwhere $E$ is some **margin of error.**\n\nLet's now discuss how to construct a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$." :tutorial/id #uuid "b64af41f-7266-5dc3-9360-aa6c591384e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6254 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "89f797af-a45c-58a5-8bec-5490a8e51df2" :step/math-academy-id 176376 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172723}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173223 :tutorial/content "We have\n\n$$\nn=40, \\widehat{\\,p} = 0.3\n$$\n\nand we wish to construct a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$.\n\nTo construct our confidence interval, the first thing to do is to establish the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$.\n\nSince the United States has around $240$ million registered voters, the sample size $n=40$ is clearly less than $10\\%$ of the population size. This means that, provided certain conditions are met, the following approximation can be applied:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's check that our usual conditions hold in this case (where we use $\\widehat{\\,p}$ as an estimate for $p$):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nnp^(^) &= 40 \\cdot 0.3 \\\\\n&= 12 \\\\\n&> 5✓ \\\\\nn(1 - p^(^)) &= 40 \\cdot (1 - 0.3) \\\\\n&= 28 \\\\\n&> 5✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all three conditions hold, the above approximation is applicable.\n\nBy transforming $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable, we have\n\n$$\nZ= \\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}\\phantom{\\mid}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nWe wish to find a $z$-interval that we're $95\\%$ confident that the random variable $Z$ lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/50/50a0f49fe5c96ba3259832fbf1a0abcedffb342301ca069bb7b88d157a181019.png)\n\nTo help with the notation, we define a new parameter $\\alpha$. Since we're computing a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nis precisely the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval. Let's label the critical values at the endpoints of our interval as $\\pm z_{\\alpha/2}$:\n\n![](images/3c/3cdfd2c72bf7fe65e5d1b584d46d4c846e74615e54c739a6d1ea990c95011a8a.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram,\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{0.025}) = P(Z < -z_{0.025}) = 0.025\n$$\n\nUsing a percentage points table for the standard normal distribution, we find that\n\n$$\nz_{0.025} \\approx 1.96\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.96) = P(Z < -1.96) = 0.025\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\nP(-1.96 < Z < 1.96) = 0.95\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is a $95\\%$ probability that our random variable $Z$ will lie in the interval $(-1.96, 1.96)$.\n\nNow, here's the trick. We solve the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the population proportion $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1.96 & < Z < 1.96 \\\\ -1.96 & < (p^(^) - p)/(\\sqrt{p^(^)(1 - p^(^))/n}) < 1.96 \\\\ -1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)} & < p^(^) - p < 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)} \\\\ -p^(^) - 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)} & < - p < - p^(^) + 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)} \\\\ p^(^) + 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)} & > p > p^(^) - 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)} \\\\ p^(^) - 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)} & < p < p^(^) + 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion ${p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\widehat{\\,p} -1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\\: \\widehat{\\,p} +1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}} \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values\n\n$$\nn=40, \\qquad \\widehat{\\,p}=0.3\n$$\n\nwe obtain that our $95\\%$ confidence interval for ${p}$ is\n\n$$\n\\big(0.3 - 0.142, \\: 0.3 + 0.142 \\big)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\big(0.158, \\: 0.442 \\big)\n$$\n\nNow that we've seen how to construct a particular confidence interval, let's discuss the more general procedure." :tutorial/id #uuid "462ecbef-51e0-5a01-808e-cd71bd17cbc2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6462 :tutorial/title "Constructing Confidence Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "413a1302-d708-5a68-8969-eee6f8bf3793" :step/math-academy-id 176377 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172724}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172724 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173213 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a sufficiently large population with sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$. Let's also assume that the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} > 5$\n- $n (1-\\widehat{\\,p}) > 5$\n\nThen, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\widehat{\\,p} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\\: \\widehat{\\,p} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}} \\right)\n$$\n\nThe endpoints of our confidence interval are the **confidence limits** and are given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n$$\n\nEach part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion\n- $p$ is the population proportion\n- $n$ is the sample size\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score\n- $E = z_{\\alpha/2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}$ is the margin of error\n\n$$\nz\n$$\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n(p^(^) - [margin of error],\\begin{vmatrix}p^(^) + [margin of error]) \\\\ (p^(^) - [[math]z[/math]-score] \\cdot [standard error], \\mid p^(^) + [[math]z[/math]-score] \\cdot [standard error]) \\\\ (p^(^) - z_{α/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)},\\end{vmatrix}p^(^) + z_{α/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)})\n$$\n\nSince computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably.\n\nFinally, in the future, you may assume that if a population is \"large,\" it is sufficiently larger than any sample drawn from it." :tutorial/id #uuid "fe719d03-f27c-50c1-8c5d-9bf9e9326e10" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6255 :tutorial/title "Confidence Intervals for Proportions"} :step/id #uuid "d86369dd-0073-526c-baf8-4f7a189e8246" :step/math-academy-id 176378 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172725}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171681 :question/id #uuid "59510c92-d9be-57c4-bc6d-497107401785" :question/math-academy-id "e-10051"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050921}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172324}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"} :step/id #uuid "2700b310-2c62-5ffd-ba65-f8b258d201e3" :step/math-academy-id 176379 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172726}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172726 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171936 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171682 :question/id #uuid "221d0cad-0cb8-5cdc-816e-19408a796d05" :question/math-academy-id "e-10054"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2b987ad-6854-59a6-bb39-c7f3c0a3db75" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172338}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions In Context"} :step/id #uuid "822975a7-7bca-5cc6-9812-46e5051e550a" :step/math-academy-id 176382}] :activity/title "Confidence Intervals for One Proportion" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172149}]}
